course: dictionary
lesson: 12003
title: Words for people who hate spending
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A cheapskate is a person who does not like spending money, even when they should. It is an informal word, so you will hear it in everyday talk and stories, not in formal writing.
explain: «Sam never pays his share of the pizza and always says he has left his wallet at home.» The clues are never pays his share and always says he has left his wallet. Those clues tell you Sam is a cheapskate.
explain: Cheapskate is an insulting word, so it can hurt someone's feelings. It is a teasing or unkind word, so use it with care. Words that mean nearly the same are stingy, tight-fisted, mean and scrooge.
fact: Cheapskate is a noun: one cheapskate, two cheapskates.
fact: Cheapskate is informal and insulting.
fact: «Cheapskate» means a person who does not like spending money, even when they should.
fact: Tightwad (US), tight-fisted, stingy and mean (UK) are similar in meaning.
fact: Scrooge is another word for someone who is a cheapskate.
exercise: «Mia never buys a round of drinks for her friends, even when it is her turn.» What is Mia? | *A cheapskate | A generous giver | A tidy cook | A champion runner
exercise: Which word means about the same as cheapskate? | Generous | *Stingy | Brave | Cheerful
exercise: Why is cheapskate an unkind word? | It is a word for a rich person | It means someone is kind and generous | *It is an insulting word, so it can hurt someone's feelings | It is a formal word used in school reports
test: «Dad said the cheapskate at the cinema had left no tip at all.» What does cheapskate mean? | *A person who does not like spending money | A person who loves cinemas | A person who is very tall | A person who sells tickets
test: Which word is NOT similar in meaning to cheapskate? | Stingy | Scrooge | *Generous | Tight-fisted
exam: Is cheapskate formal or informal, and is it kind or unkind? | Formal and unkind | Informal and kind | Formal and kind | *Informal and unkind
